---
kicker: "09"
title: "Fireplaces"
dek: "Explain the energy and indoor-air-quality risks of open fireplaces and recommend sealed alternatives."
---

Based on the project drawings, it appears a fireplace is planned for the home. While we understand the aesthetic and
lifestyle benefits of these appliances, we strongly recommend against open fireplaces in any home. Open-hearth
fireplaces are a heating energy penalty, cooling the building through increased infiltration both when in use and when
not. Even more significant are the potential health impacts: open fireplaces contribute to poor indoor air quality, with
a higher risk of unhealthy NO<sub>x</sub> and CO levels and high particulate matter, which can exacerbate respiratory
problems for occupants.

If an open fireplace is used, occupants should understand the potential health effects and take measures to reduce the
risk as far as possible. In particular, occupants should follow the HPBA responsible wood-burning guidelines, which
include:

- Never leave a fire unattended. Extinguish any fire completely before leaving the room or going to bed.
- Ensure the home has multiple working CO detectors near the fireplace.
- Inspect and maintain the chimney regularly.
- Monitor smoke development in the home carefully.
- Use only dry, seasoned wood, and softwood instead of hardwood for smaller, more manageable fires.
- Where possible, ensure the fireplace equipment meets the EPA certification rules.
- Consider running power to the top of the chimney for future installation of a draft inducer.
- Never use any venting appliances (dryers, kitchen hood) while the fireplace is in use. This could depressurize the home
  and back-draft smoke and contaminants down the chimney.

Follow all local code requirements for construction and venting, in particular [IRC
1001.7.1](https://codes.iccsafe.org/content/IRC2018/chapter-10-chimneys-and-fireplaces) (dampers), [IRC
1006](https://codes.iccsafe.org/content/IRC2018/chapter-10-chimneys-and-fireplaces) (exterior combustion air), and [IECC
R402.4.2](https://codes.iccsafe.org/content/IECC2018P4/chapter-4-re-residential-energy-efficiency) (tight-fitting flue
dampers or doors and outdoor combustion air).

<div class="btwr-callout">
    In place of open fireplaces, we recommend sealed units, which can achieve a similar aesthetic while providing better
    controllability and dedicated combustion air, reducing the risk of negative indoor-air-quality effects.
</div>

Due to the efficient design of the home, use the smallest firebox that meets the design goals. Even the smallest unit
available will likely be oversized for the home's heating need, and occupants may need to open a window during use to
avoid overheating. Prioritize units with good air sealing and tight-fitting front doors, and provide dedicated
combustion air via ducting to each unit. Where possible, units should meet the [EPA woodstove
certification](https://cfpub.epa.gov/oarweb/woodstove/index.cfm?fuseaction=app.search) requirements and [EPA
recommendations](https://www.epa.gov/burnwise/choosing-wood-burning-appliances). Some suitable manufacturers include:

- [Jøtul](https://www.jotul.com/products/wood)
- [RAIS](https://us.rais.com/stoves/)
- [Stûv](https://www.stuvamerica.com/)
- [Wittus](https://wittus.com/wood-built-ins/)

